iences training and opportunities for  black and brown youth i had an amazing  life in tokyo but what i didn't have was  a sense of purpose as a young man who  grew up in tulsa i knew that it was my  responsibility my obligation really to  come home and provide those same  opportunities in their classes the guild  teaches middle and high school students  how to build websites create apps and  digital games last year we did the black  wall street business directory super  proud of that that project kettle spent  about half a year learning how to build  a website html css a bit of java we put  them all together and we created a  website that  focused on all the businesses that  existed in historic greenwood to really  celebrate those entrepreneurs  what they were able to build and then  imagining if those businesses existed  today they would most likely have a  website  the kiddos are building websites they're  building mobile apps they're building  their own games and this really just  isn't something that our our schools  have the capacity to teach right now we  want to do something fun and innovative  and make sure that this is available to  kids across our school district and  initiatives like vaughn's aren't the  only to have recognized education as the  key to making the vision of tulsa as a  leader for tech a reality i  love working with entrepreneurs i love  knowing what people are dreaming about  taylor is another native tolson in her  role she provides education for  entrepreneurs specifically from black  brown and under-resourced communities  and i think one of the most rewarding  experiences for me is when someone sits  in my office and says  wow like i didn't even know  that i was this close a lot of times  when i'm working with  under-resourced entrepreneurs  no one is thinking about how to scale  their business into 15 different states  right away people are thinking  how do i make money on just one or two  customers and being able to say well hey  do you know this person hey did you know  that we have this loan program let's put  it all on the board and see what are  your multiple options here together  taylor vaughn and billingsley form part  of what is becoming a budding ecosystem  of support support that has been sorely  lacking in this community the origin of  actually black tech street is i begin to  ask myself the question you know what  could black wall street have been had it  been supported and not destroyed and you  know looking at the level of tenacity  that it took for these black  entrepreneurs to build these incredible  businesses during jim crow that smashing  through walls thinking outside of the  box it reminded me a lot of the tech  industry and when i coupled that with  the fact that technology is one of the  only industries you can build  intergenerational wealth in seven to ten  years in and that by the year 2030 there  will be 4.3 million vacant tech jobs due  to a tech talent shortage i not only saw  an incredible wealth building  opportunity for black people i kind of  saw the black wall street vision pushed  to a new horizon  how do we stimulate  the wealth and the essence in the spirit  that used to live here  and  what i have found personally in the work  that i'm doing is that the spirit still  is here all eyes are on us so what a  better time than now
 

